00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.730
- It is no cliche that if you
work for a consulting firm

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.707
you will likely create lots of slides.

00:00:04.707 --> 00:00:06.360
And so in the six years I worked

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.140
for the consulting firm, McKinsey,

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.427
I probably created really
thousands of PowerPoint pages.

00:00:11.427 --> 00:00:14.150
And so today's coffee
break here on my channel,

00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:17.000
I wanna share some additional
tips on exactly the topic

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.680
and specifically on the
thing that arguably is

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.720
both the most important
part of slide writing

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.710
but also the most challenging
part of slide writing

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.360
in the life of anyone working
in professional services.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:29.780
And this is creating charts

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:32.550
and diagrams that first, look professional

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:35.020
and then also communicate your message

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:37.680
in an effective and concise
and to the point way

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.913
I'm very proud that this video
is sponsored by think-cell.

00:00:39.913 --> 00:00:42.790
Think-cell is a plugin
for PowerPoint and Excel

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:45.620
that is used by most of
the large consulting firms.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:48.640
And I probably use it
literally every single day

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:50.200
but more about them in a moment.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.070
So the plan for this
video is the following.

00:00:52.070 --> 00:00:54.490
First, I'm going to talk
about chart formatting

00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:56.180
so how to make sure that your charts

00:00:56.180 --> 00:00:57.680
really look professional.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.940
Second, I'm going to talk
about chart selection.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.800
One of the most important
things to get right

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.860
is really to choose the right
chart type for your data.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:06.980
And we're going to do this
with some little exercises.

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:08.660
So it's going to be very hands on.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:09.800
And the last but not least,

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:12.100
I'm going to talk about
some typical errors

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.530
that people employ when
they work with charts

00:01:14.530 --> 00:01:16.970
with data in creating slides.

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:18.950
So without further do let's get into it,

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:20.740
let's talk about chart formatting.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:22.890
So let's look at this example of a chart.

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:26.130
Let's just look at the
formatting, so how this looks.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:27.370
What do you think?

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:29.390
Is this chart looking professional?

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:31.310
Should you make a chart
that looks like this?

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:33.600
And feel free to pause maybe for a moment

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:37.000
and potentially think about
how you could improve this.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:38.960
So take a couple of seconds if you like

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:42.410
and now I wanna improve this
chart together with you.

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:44.380
And the first thing that
likely comes to mind

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:46.660
is all these colors in the chart.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.180
So should you really use
that many different colors

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:50.969
for all these different bars?

00:01:50.969 --> 00:01:54.040
(indistinct) general idea is
that there's nothing wrong

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:56.000
with using colors in your chart.

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:59.680
You should just make sure
that colors have a meaning,

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:03.040
colors should convey a
message, then you can use them.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:04.410
You shouldn't just use colors

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.330
for the sake of making
everything colorful.

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:07.460
And so here for this chart,

00:02:07.460 --> 00:02:10.930
I would recommend you to
rather use toned down colors,

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:14.610
very basic colors because I'd
argue all these colors here

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:17.480
don't really help you to
bring across any message.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.310
If at all it is this directing
from what you wanna show.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:22.820
And then maybe you can set an accent,

00:02:22.820 --> 00:02:26.200
a color accent on the latest
year to stress that this is

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:27.050
the newest number.

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:29.250
Here in the time series, the latest figure

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:31.700
that you maybe also wanna
focus on with your chart.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:33.370
The next thing that might come to mind

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:35.660
is all these lines that you see here.

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.900
It just looks very crowded,
very busy, even though actually

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:41.930
the numbers that you're trying
to show here are quite basic

00:02:41.930 --> 00:02:42.763
and simple.

00:02:42.763 --> 00:02:44.870
So probably the first
thing I wanna take out,

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:47.770
all these connector
lines between the bars,

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:50.070
I'd argue they don't really add any value

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:51.530
at all here in this chart.

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:53.250
And the same thing is likely also true

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.658
for all these (indistinct)
lines, all these grid lines

00:02:55.658 --> 00:02:57.030
in the background.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:58.806
And here I'd argue what I'd take out

00:02:58.806 --> 00:03:01.740
is also the Y axis to the side.

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:04.440
In this example, you
already have all the numbers

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.320
on top of the bars.

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:08.630
Why do you need to show
this additional axis

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:10.540
take it out and you will be very fine.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.462
And now let's also look at the X axis

00:03:12.462 --> 00:03:15.580
because it comes with
a very bold, dominant

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:18.750
dark black that really
dominates the picture.

00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:20.200
So I'd say it's not really helpful

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:22.160
because of course you
want rather the numbers

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:25.730
the bars to stand out and
not this very broad X axis.

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:27.620
So let's lighten it up a little bit.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.860
And now what I would encourage you to add

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:32.826
are rather some arrows,
different arrows, some elements

00:03:32.826 --> 00:03:36.180
that really help you to
bring out the message

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:37.287
that you wanna bring out.

00:03:37.287 --> 00:03:38.930
And of course, what exactly that is,

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:40.810
it will depend on indeed, the message

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:42.440
that you do wanna communicate

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:44.544
but maybe you wanna show
how the revenues increased

00:03:44.544 --> 00:03:47.350
from 2015 to 2021.

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:50.190
Maybe this is what you would want to show.

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:51.760
And if this is indeed the case,

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.090
why don't include a difference arrow

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:57.890
that shows how the
revenue increased by 400%

00:03:57.890 --> 00:04:01.260
from 2015 into 2021.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:02.820
And then this will potentially be a number

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:05.952
that you could also cite and
refer to in the action title.

00:04:05.952 --> 00:04:08.110
So if you don't know
what action titles are,

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:10.480
also a very important
part of slide writing,

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:11.800
I will link another video that I did

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:13.740
on this very topic above here.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.680
So one key concept to have in mind here

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:18.860
is the idea of the data-to-ink ratio.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:21.750
The idea is that this ratio
should be as high as possible.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:23.730
So ideally you're able to show

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:28.111
as much data as possible with
as little ink as possible.

00:04:28.111 --> 00:04:30.919
An implication of this is
that you should just take out,

00:04:30.919 --> 00:04:34.530
delete all the additional
filler lines, (indistinct) lines

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:36.903
background lines, axes
and so on and so forth.

00:04:36.903 --> 00:04:40.300
Unless of course they really
needed and really add value

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:42.550
to the a message that
you want to bring across.

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:44.490
So always think data-to-ink ratio

00:04:44.490 --> 00:04:47.810
and this alone will already
improve your charts, I promise.

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:50.030
Next, let's talk about
choosing the right chart time

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:51.910
for the data that you want to visualize

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:54.751
because this idea is of more
complicated than it might look.

00:04:54.751 --> 00:04:57.490
So let's start with this
very simple exercise here.

00:04:57.490 --> 00:05:00.160
You see different products
and how much revenue

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.463
they contribute to the
total revenue of a company.

00:05:02.463 --> 00:05:05.180
So the sum of them all
is a hundred percent.

00:05:05.180 --> 00:05:08.280
So what chart type would you
choose to visualize this?

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:10.300
And again, feel free to
take a couple of seconds.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:12.930
Maybe pause the video to
think about this indeed.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:14.720
Now let's look at a couple of examples

00:05:14.720 --> 00:05:16.310
because at least from my experience,

00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:18.940
the chart type that people
will mention most frequently

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:21.490
in the situation is a pie chart.

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:23.730
So we should surely use a pie chart

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:25.760
to visualize this, right?

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:27.283
But here what you directly
see in this example

00:05:27.283 --> 00:05:31.780
is that this pie chart looks
quite messy, quite overcrowded.

00:05:31.780 --> 00:05:33.900
The basic rule of thumb
is that whenever you have

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.610
more than about five elements to show,

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:39.300
pie charts are quite
difficult to work with

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:41.640
because the elements are
just getting too small

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:43.850
to really display them in
a visually appealing way.

00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:46.130
But then also at least if you
read the literature on that,

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:49.400
just interpreting the area of an element

00:05:49.400 --> 00:05:52.720
does not really seem to be too
intuitive to the human mind.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:54.670
So is it really that clear
to you that for instance,

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:57.080
product nine is twice the share,

00:05:57.080 --> 00:05:59.260
twice the size of product 10

00:05:59.260 --> 00:06:02.280
or product five is twice
the size of product nine.

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:04.710
So these relative differences in area

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:08.390
are not that natural to
grasp and to understand

00:06:08.390 --> 00:06:10.490
and often about bar charts like this

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:11.620
as shown here on the right

00:06:11.620 --> 00:06:13.900
might be a bit more of an intuitive way

00:06:13.900 --> 00:06:16.767
of also really comparing even
relative numbers like this.

00:06:16.767 --> 00:06:18.043
And the key point that I wanna make here

00:06:18.043 --> 00:06:20.840
is that in the beginning
of visualizing data,

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:22.310
always make it an effort of yours.

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:24.620
So thinking about what
the options might be

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:25.892
and what really the best choice

00:06:25.892 --> 00:06:27.490
of a chart might be for that.

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:29.509
And of course, some chart
types just work better

00:06:29.509 --> 00:06:32.380
for some specific types
of data than for others.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:34.590
So here, for instance,
a couple of arch types.

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:36.420
So component comparison.

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:39.310
So relative comparison, here, pie charts

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:41.660
or stacked column charts
might work quite well.

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:44.560
So for things of share of,
percentage of and so on,

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:46.840
if it's more about an absolute comparison,

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:49.810
then you maybe want to go
for the vertical bar charts

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:51.050
or the waterfall charts.

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:53.210
For time series of course line charts

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:54.810
or column charts might work.

00:06:54.810 --> 00:06:57.120
So if you really wanna show
how a certain time series

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:00.390
develops over time, then of
course they have distributions,

00:07:00.390 --> 00:07:02.480
correlations and so on and so forth.

00:07:02.480 --> 00:07:04.070
I would actually
encourage you to print out

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:06.930
this specific slide and potentially
keep it as a cheat sheet

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:07.763
next to your desk.

